solution_steps:
We can use the Euclidean algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of the two numbers.
\begin{align*}
\gcd(83^9+1,83^9+83^2+1) &= \gcd(83^9+1-(83^9+83^2+1),83^9+83^2+1) \\
&= \gcd(-83^2,83^9+83^2+1).
\end{align*}
Since $83^2$ is a factor of $83^9+83^2+1$, we have
\begin{align*}
\gcd(-83^2,83^9+83^2+1) &= \gcd(-83^2,83^9+83^2+1-83^9) \\
&= \gcd(-83^2,83^2+1).
\end{align*}
Again, $83^2$ is a factor of $83^2+1$, so we have
\begin{align*}
\gcd(-83^2,83^2+1) &= \gcd(-83^2,83^2+1-(-83^2)) \\
&= \gcd(-83^2,2).
\end{align*}
Since $83^2$ is relatively prime to $2$, the gcd is simply $\boxed{1}$.The answer is: 1

